186424225 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186424199 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424225 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186424205 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424225 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186424210 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424225 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186424211 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424225 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186424213 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424225 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424225 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424225 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186424211 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424225 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "fadbbb52-eaa9-5960-9164-0e7ea16514af" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424226 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186424225 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424226 #edb/ref 1069 "q-123172" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424226 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424226 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $F=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,i-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,j,$ what is $\\text{div}\\,F?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424226 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "162e9632-ec96-53eb-bf5e-ab2bd97ae9e0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424226 #edb/ref 1074 "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the divergence of $F$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n\\text{div}\\,F=∇\\cdot F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot F & =\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{x-y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424227 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186424224 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424227 #edb/ref 1069 "q-131195" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424227 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424227 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/39/39dfec02c787141d8a728381f4093dfe9cbf3fdbf996177ba6f24d973015dc5f.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true everywhere in the domain of $F?$\n\nI. $∇\\cdot F=0$\n\nII. $∇\\cdot F>0$\n\nIII. $∇\\cdot F<0$\n\nIV. $F$ is solenoidal" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424227 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5777673f-cba1-5775-ae01-f18d4531f239" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424227 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that $\\text{div}\\,F=∇\\cdot F$ denotes the divergence of the vector field $F.$\n\nIntuitively, the divergence of $F$ at a point $P$ measures how much $F$ acts like a source or a sink at $P:$\n\n- If $F$ acts like a source at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F>0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $F$ acts like a sink at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F<0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $F$ acts like neither a source nor a sink at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F=0$ at $P.$\n\n![](images/6c/6c747149618d4c5ef35e727af15cd001365bd9c2d5e064689baf24c6ee23cff9.png)\n\nIf $∇\\cdot F=0$ everywhere in the domain of $F,$ we say that $F$ is solenoidal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statement II is true, whereas statements I and III are false. Notice that the lengths of the vector's $y$-components increase as we move vertically away from the origin. Therefore, the vector field $F$ acts like a source at every point.\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since $∇\\cdot F\\ne 0,$ the vector field $F$ is not solenoidal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424228 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186424223 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424228 #edb/ref 1069 "q-131196" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424228 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424228 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/4b/4bf8de7e8719545b3d8abf5dc9fb4bc0c5f71034dba7186b7d9822d9e8be044d.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true everywhere in the domain of $F?$\n\nI. $∇\\cdot F=0$\n\nII. $∇\\cdot F>0$\n\nIII. $∇\\cdot F<0$\n\nIV. $F$ is solenoidal" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424228 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9278ec55-4908-5c1c-8a74-640611473537" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424228 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that $\\text{div}\\,F=∇\\cdot F$ denotes the divergence of the vector field $F.$\n\nIntuitively, the divergence of $F$ at a point $P$ measures how much $F$ acts like a source or a sink at $P:$\n\n- If $F$ acts like a source at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F>0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $F$ acts like a sink at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F<0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $F$ acts like neither a source nor a sink at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F=0$ at $P.$\n\n![](images/6c/6c747149618d4c5ef35e727af15cd001365bd9c2d5e064689baf24c6ee23cff9.png)\n\nIf $∇\\cdot F=0$ everywhere in the domain of $F,$ we say that $F$ is solenoidal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statement III is true, whereas statements I and II are false. Notice that the lengths of the vector's $x$-components decrease as we move horizontally towards the origin. Therefore, the vector field $F$ acts like a sink at every point.\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since $∇\\cdot F\\ne 0,$ the vector field $F$ is not solenoidal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424229 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186424222 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424229 #edb/ref 1069 "q-105562" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424229 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424229 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the vector field $F={y}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,i+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y\\,j.$ \nEvaluate $\\text{div}\\,F$ at the point $(\\pi ,1).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424229 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ca50a185-e281-53e0-900c-05cc2ac6e7a5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424229 #edb/ref 1074 "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the divergence of $F$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n\\text{div}\\,F=∇\\cdot F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot F & =\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) \\\\  & =2{y}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+\\frac{x}{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate $\\text{div}\\,F$ at the point $(\\pi ,1),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(∇\\cdot F)(\\pi ,1) & =2(1)^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi )+\\frac{\\pi }{1} \\\\  & =2(1)(1)+\\pi  \\\\  & =2+\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424230 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186424221 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424230 #edb/ref 1069 "q-123196" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424230 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424230 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the vector field $F=(x+y)^{2}\\,i+(y+z)^{2}\\,j+(x+z)^{2}\\,k.$ Evaluate $\\text{div}\\,F$ at the point $(-2,1,-1).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424230 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "df216990-7a71-5841-8a5b-955dc6939d8d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424230 #edb/ref 1074 "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j+R\\,k$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the divergence of $F$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n\\text{div}\\,F=∇\\cdot F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot F & =\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}((x+y)^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}((y+z)^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂z}((x+z)^{2}) \\\\  & =2(x+y)+2(y+z)+2(x+z) \\\\  & =4(x+y+z).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate $\\text{div}\\,F$ at the point $(-2,1,-1),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F(-2,1,-1) & =4((-2)+(1)+(-1)) \\\\  & =4\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
